  "@authEmailLabel": {
    "description": "The email field's accessible name, which `EvaTextField` puts in `Semantics(label:)`. Transcribed: redesign.html:506 — `placeholder=\"email\"`; the redesign's field has no `<label>`, so the placeholder word is the name a browser would read and the word carried here."
  },
  "@authEmailHint": {
    "description": "The email field's visible placeholder. Transcribed: redesign.html:506 — `placeholder=\"email\"`.\n\nLOCALIZED ON THE ARABIC ARM, unlike `you@example.com` before it. The old placeholder was an ASCII-domain sample string that no language translates; this one is a common noun naming what the reader should type, and the redesign's own dictionary translates every word a reader acts on (`emErr` carries `البريد`). The Arabic is the standard form, and it is the same noun `authEmailLabel` carries — placeholder and accessible name agree, as a browser's would."
  },
  "@authPasswordLabel": {
    "description": "The password field's accessible name. Transcribed: redesign.html:507 — `placeholder=\"password\"`; see `authEmailLabel` for why the placeholder word is the name."
  },
  "@authPasswordHint": {
    "description": "The password field's visible placeholder. Transcribed: redesign.html:507 — `placeholder=\"password\"`, replacing the masked `••••••••` the React prototype drew.\n\nA WORD AND NOT A MASK, and that is the redesign's choice, not a change of mind about masks: the placeholder names the field the way a browser's `placeholder=\"password\"` does, and `authPasswordLabel` carries the same noun. Deliberately localized on the Arabic arm; the word is `كلمة المرور`, which is the exact noun `pwErr` (`redesign.html:976`) already carries — no new translation is invented."
  },
  "@authEnterTheWord": {
    "description": "The sign-in CTA. Transcribed: redesign.html:508 — `data-i18n=\"enter\"`, one of the THREE strings on the redesigned login the prototype itself translates (`redesign.html:965` — `enter:[\"enter the word\",\"ادخل الكلمة\"]`). The Arabic is the prototype's own, not a new translation."
  },
  "@authEmailInvalid": {
    "description": "The email field's error line. Transcribed: redesign.html:976 — `emErr:[\"email looks incomplete\",\"البريد غير مكتمل\"]`, announced by `redesign.html:1305`'s one regex test. The Arabic is the prototype's own.\n\nRENDERED BY PRESENCE, NOT BY MATCHING: the page draws this sentence when `state.emailError != null` and never compares the domain's message text, so the wording in `login_credentials.dart` is the log-canonical copy and this key is the reader's."
  },
  "@authPasswordTooShort": {
    "description": "The password field's error line. Transcribed: redesign.html:976 — `pwErr:[\"password too short\",\"كلمة المرور قصيرة\"]`, announced by `redesign.html:1306`'s one length test. Rendered by presence, as `authEmailInvalid` says."
  },
  "@authUnavailableSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to a permanently-disabled control's name, so a screen reader announces WHY it cannot be pressed instead of only that it cannot.\n\nNAMESPACED `auth*` because **FIVE** features carry a field of this name and they are NOT all the same string: `/quiz`'s says there is no answer to show, and the other four say the same thing. See `quizUnavailableSuffix`.\n\nAPPENDED BY THE CALL SITE, NEVER BAKED IN — the two inert ghost pills on `/login` share labels, and a suffix inside the label would render \"unavailable in this build - unavailable in this build\"."
  },
  "@homeGreetingMorning": {
    "description": "05:00–11:59. Transcribed: `redesign.html:926` — `morn:[\"good morning\",\"صباح النور\"]`, set by `applyLang` (`:988`).\n\n**The Arabic arm changed with the screen.** It was `صباح الخير` — “good morning” in the ordinary sense — because the old greeting was a status line. The redesign's own word is `صباح النور` (“morning of light”), which is a different sentence, and transcribing it is the point of the phase.\n\nThe English value keeps its capital: the run is a `.mono` and `[dir=rtl] .mono` at `redesign.html:136` removes the transform only on the Arabic arm, so under LTR both spellings paint as `GOOD MORNING`."
  },
  "@homeGreetingAfternoon": {
    "description": "12:00–17:59. Transcribed: `redesign.html:926` — `noon:[\"good afternoon\",\"نهارك نور\"]`. See `homeGreetingMorning` for why the Arabic arm changed, and note the prototype's own grammar: `نهارك نور` is second-person (`your day is light`) where the English is an adjective phrase. Transcribed as written."
  },
  "@homeGreetingEvening": {
    "description": "18:00–23:59. Transcribed: `redesign.html:926` — `eve:[\"good evening\",\"مساء النور\"]`. See `homeGreetingMorning` for why the Arabic arm changed.\n\n**The bucket's end is the redesign's**: `hourKey()` (`:983-984`) returns `eve` for every hour of the evening and sends 00:00–04:59 to `vigil`, where the old three-bucket clock sent them to evening."
  },
  "@homeGreetingSeparator": {
    "description": "What follows `homePeace` when a name comes after it.\n\n**Its subject is the greeting's second line now**, where it used to be the old lead-in's: `redesign.html:993` writes `'سلام، مريم'` / `'peace, Mariam'`, and the comma is this key.\n\nA KEY AND NOT A CHARACTER IN CODE, because it is the one place the two arms genuinely differ and it is the difference a translator would otherwise have to find by reading the greeting code. English takes `, `; Arabic takes `، ` — U+060C ARABIC COMMA, a DIFFERENT codepoint from U+002C which renders at a different height. Substituting one for the other produces text that is right and looks wrong, which is the failure mode a bilingual string table exists to prevent."
  },
  "@homeStreakLabel": {
    "description": "The streak count's accessible name, as a PREFIX. `StreakChip.semanticLabel`.\n\nA PREFIX AND NOT A PLURALISED PHRASE, and the reason is recorded rather than assumed: the announcement is composed by the chip as `$semanticLabel: $days $daysLabel`. Converting this to an ICU plural would change the announcement's SHAPE from “Streak: 5 days” to “5-day streak”, which is a UX change and not a localization one. The Arabic value is already a PLURAL NOUN (`أيام متتالية`, “consecutive days”), so a count beside it is grammatically sound; the painted unit beside the number is `homeDays`. See `readingCaptionFor` for a count that IS genuinely plural-dependent."
  },
  "@homeUnavailableSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to a control's accessible name while the control is inert — the library's book cards and the selah circle.\n\n**Its subject was the avatar's and the avatar is gone.** `redesign.html:513-538` draws no avatar on `/`; the top bar that carried it was `ds.tsx:499-530`, which the redesign replaced with the greeting block. The wording is `authUnavailableSuffix`'s and the reasoning is its own: a screen reader should be told WHY the control cannot be pressed, not only that it cannot. NAMESPACED `home*` because **five** features carry this field name and `/quiz`'s differs."
  },
  "@homeRetry": {
    "description": "`ErrorView.retryLabel`. There is NO prototype error state at all — `eva/src` has none, because it has no data layer that can fail — so this is WRITTEN, and it says WHAT IT DOES (\"Try again\") rather than \"Retry\".\n\nNAMESPACED `home*`: three features carry this field name (`homeRetry`, `quizRetry`, `readingRetry`).\n\n`ErrorView` is the precedent for all three: a hard-coded English string inside `core/` is the half-translated UI this app exists not to ship, so the wording lives with the caller and the widget takes it."
  },
  "@quizExit": {
    "description": "The back control's accessible name.\n\nREWORDED WITH THE CHROME IT NAMES. The old value (\"Close\") named a cross icon; the redesign replaces that control with the `.chev` chevron (`redesign.html:564`), whose click returns to the reader (`:1412` — `qBack` is `pop('reader')` then `enterReader()`, a back step and not a dismissal). The client's control is still `context.router.maybePop()`, which is the same move.\n\nStill WRITTEN, NOT TRANSCRIBED: `:564` is a `<button>` around a bare `‹` with NO LABEL AT ALL — §14's first row, verbatim. The same obligation that forced `readingBack` forces this."
  },
  "@quizRefraction": {
    "description": "The crumb's first half — `Refraction · <reference>`.\n\nTranscribed from the prototype's I18N row (`redesign.html:967`, `refraction:[\"Refraction\",\"انعطاف\"]`), rendered at `:565` as the appbar's centred `#qCrumb` and filled from the reader's own passage (`:1348` — `` `${tr('refraction')} · ${bookTitle(b)}` ``). The client's second half is the payload's `reference`, carried on `QuizSession` and composed in `quiz_l10n.dart` — an ARB placeholder would fail the Latin/numeral gates, the `home_l10n.dart` precedent."
  },
  "@quizProgress": {
    "description": "`ProgressBeads`' accessible-name prefix for the re-homed dots row.\n\nREWORDED WITH ITS JOB. The old value (\"Question\") was the noun of the composed `Question 2 of 5` line, which the redesign's quiz screen does not have — its only progress surface is the `.dots` row (`redesign.html:568`, CSS `:328-332`). The row is a bare unlabelled `<div>` in the prototype, so this name is WRITTEN, NOT TRANSCRIBED — §14's colour-and-shape row needs the progress announced, and `ProgressBeads`' `Semantics` is where it lands.\n\nSemantics-only, never painted: `ProgressBeads` puts it in `Semantics(label:, excludeSemantics: true)`, so the Arabic-typography gate declines to declare it."
  },
  "@quizCheckAnswer": {
    "description": "The CTA while a choice is made and not yet graded.\n\nTRANSCRIBED FROM `QuizScreen.tsx:127`, AND THE VALUE IS STILL THE ONE IT WROTE. The superseded prototype's switch was `{checked ? 'Next question' : 'Check answer'}`; this is its unchecked arm, unchanged. The redesign has no such label at all: its grader is one-tap (`redesign.html:1423` — `pick` grades the instant a card is chosen), so `.qnext` only ever appears graded (`:1421` removes it; `:1430` shows it). This client's grade is a `POST` (`POST /readings/:id/submit`, §5 trap 10 — no successful submit may be fired on a mis-tap), so choosing and grading stay two presses and the second press needs a name the prototype never wrote.\n\nThe citation is kept because the value is kept: the step is the same, so the line that named it still does."
  },
  "@quizNextQuestion": {
    "description": "The CTA after the answer is graded, with a question still to come.\n\nREWORDING, ON A MEASUREMENT. The old value (\"Next question\") was the superseded prototype's switch label. The redesign's own I18N row is `next:[\"next ▸\",\"التالي\"]` (`redesign.html:967`), and `.qnext` renders it mono, uppercase, tracked (`:364`) — so the rendered form is `NEXT ▸` exactly as the prototype draws it. The `▸` (U+25B8) is safe as TEXT on the English arm only because `.qnext` is mono (`:364`) and JetBrainsMono carries the codepoint — measured against all nineteen bundled TTFs, same instrument as `font_coverage.dart`. The Arabic value carries no arrow, which is the prototype's own pair.\n\nThe terminal arm took the redesign's other label — see `quizSeeResults`."
  },
  "@quizSeeResults": {
    "description": "The CTA on the last graded question — the terminal arm.\n\nNOW TRANSCRIBED, AND THE VALUE REWORDED TO THE REDESIGN'S OWN. The old description called this key written because the old prototype had no terminal state. The redesign does: its I18N row is `seeRes:[\"see results ✦\",\"النتائج\"]` (`redesign.html:967`), applied at `:1431` (`label = last question ? tr('seeRes') : tr('next')`), and one click opens the results (`:1432`).\n\nTHE ✦ IS NEVER TEXT. U+2726 is in NO bundled family — measured against all nineteen TTFs (`four_pointed_star.dart` carries the measurement) — so the star is the painted `FourPointedStar` composed by the CTA on the English arm, and the Arabic arm carries none, which is the prototype's own pair. The value here is therefore the words only."
  },
  "@quizCorrectSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to the correct option's accessible name once it is revealed.\n\nTHE SPOILER BOUNDARY'S VOCABULARY, AND IT IS BILINGUAL. The boundary itself is `QuizPage`'s; this is the word that makes it visible to a screen reader.\n\nA CITATION THAT USED TO NAME A FILE THAT DID NOT EXIST, AND THE CLAIM IS HELD TWO FILES OVER INSTEAD. An earlier version of this doc said `quiz_accessibility_test.dart` asserts the string is in the semantics tree only after a check. There is no such file and there never was. The claim is nevertheless real and is held by `quiz_page_test.dart`: its \"before the reader commits to an answer\" group asserts no node's label carries this string, and its \"the SEMANTICS tree carries the verdict the card colours carry\" test asserts exactly one does after a check.\n\nNOT CORRECTED BY CREATING THE FILE. A second /quiz test file whose subject is a subset of that spoiler group would give the boundary two homes and make \"nothing leaks before a check\" a claim about whichever file a reader opened."
  },
  "@quizIncorrectSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to the chosen-but-wrong option's accessible name, once revealed."
  },
  "@quizAlreadyAnsweredSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to an option's accessible name when the wire says it is answered.\n\n§14's disabled row: a reader has to be told WHY a control cannot be pressed, not only that it cannot. `LoginPage`'s four inert social buttons and `AppTopBar`'s avatar are the precedent, and they are the reason /quiz can ship a dead CTA honestly at all."
  },
  "@quizUnavailableSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to the CTA's accessible name when there is nothing to submit.\n\nNAMESPACED `quiz*` BECAUSE THIS IS NOT THE SAME STRING AS THE OTHER FOUR. `authUnavailableSuffix`, `homeUnavailableSuffix`, `readingUnavailableSuffix` and `journeyUnavailableSuffix` all say \"unavailable in this build\"; this one says why *this* control is unavailable, which is §14's actual requirement and a different fact."
  },
  "@quizRetry": {
    "description": "`ErrorView.retryLabel`. Written — the prototype has no data layer, so it has no error state and nothing to transcribe. NAMESPACED `quiz*`; see `homeRetry` for the three-way collision."
  },
  "@quizNoQuestionsTitle": {
    "description": "The empty state's heading.\n\nWRITTEN, NOT TRANSCRIBED, BECAUSE THE STATE IS REACHABLE: `questions: []` is reachable — `today_reading_mapper.dart` SKIPS an unreadable question rather than refusing the passage (recorded decision 50), and decision 70 already gates /reading's CTA on `questionCount > 0`. The prototype has no such state."
  },
  "@quizNoQuestionsMessage": {
    "description": "The empty state's body. Written, for `quizNoQuestionsTitle`'s reason."
  },
  "@readingBack": {
    "description": "The back control's accessible name.\n\nWRITTEN, NOT TRANSCRIBED: ReadingEnScreen.tsx:14-16 and ReadingArScreen.tsx:26-28 are `<button>`s wrapping an inline `<svg>` chevron with nothing at all — §14's first row, verbatim."
  },
  "@readingTextSize": {
    "description": "The `Aa` control's accessible name.\n\n**NOT `Aa`.** §14's first row lists `Aa` among the icon-only buttons that have no accessible name, and `IconActionButton`'s own doc names it as one of \"the `Aa` and bookmark controls Phase 7 composes\" — which is why this control is that widget with a tooltip rather than a text button whose label is its own name.\n\nA SEPARATE KEY FROM `readingFontSize`, AND A TEST PROVED IT. The first version reused one string for both, on the reasoning that \"they name the same control from two positions\". They do not: `readingTextSize` names the DISCLOSURE YOU PRESS and `readingFontSize` names THE SLIDER THE DISCLOSURE REVEALS — two different nodes, both focusable-adjacent, both announced. Sharing one string put two nodes on screen with the identical label, which is a §14 failure in the one place §14 is unambiguous: a screen-reader user hears \"Text size\" twice and cannot tell the button from the thing it opened. `reading_accessibility_test.dart` caught it in the failing direction — `_nodeLabelled` found the button's node instead of the slider's and the `isSlider` flag was false."
  },
  "@readingFontSize": {
    "description": "The `FontSizeStepper`'s track, as the SLIDER's own accessible name. The Arabic pair is `حجم الخط` (the button) and `مقياس حجم الخط` (the scale itself) — see `readingTextSize`'s description for the test that forced these to be two keys."
  },
  "@readingDecreaseFontSize": {
    "description": "The `FontSizeStepper`'s decrement button, as an `IconActionButton` tooltip and accessible name.\n\nWRITTEN HERE AND NOT LEFT IN THE DESIGN SYSTEM AS A LITERAL, which is what it was: `FontSizeStepper` hard-coded 'Decrease font size' and 'Increase font size', so a reader who opened the `Aa` panel on the Arabic arm was told, IN ENGLISH, what the two buttons beside them did. `ErrorView.retryLabel` is the precedent for the alternative — a design-system widget that renders a caller's script takes the caller's string, because a hard-coded English string in `core/` is the half-translated UI this app exists not to ship.\n\nAND THE PROTOTYPE HAS NO WORDING TO TRANSCRIBE: SettingsScreen.tsx:66-68 is a bare range input with `-` and `+` and no labels at all."
  },
  "@readingIncreaseFontSize": {
    "description": "The `FontSizeStepper`'s increment button. Written, for `readingDecreaseFontSize`'s reason."
  },
  "@readingBookmark": {
    "description": "The bookmark control's accessible name, BEFORE any suffix. The bookmark has no endpoint and no port — ReadingEnScreen.tsx:19-21 draws one with no handler — so it ships INERT and says so via `readingUnavailableSuffix`."
  },
  "@readingUnavailableSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to a control's name while that control is inert.\n\nThe same wording, and the same reasoning, as `authUnavailableSuffix`, `homeUnavailableSuffix` and `journeyUnavailableSuffix`: a reader should be told WHY a control cannot be pressed, not only that it cannot. NAMESPACED `reading*`; `/quiz`'s is a different string, and `/journey`'s is the same as this one."
  },
  "@readingPassage": {
    "description": "The name `ScriptureBlock` gives its merged semantics node, so a screen-reader user arriving at the reading body hears WHAT they have entered rather than a bare collection of verses.\n\nTranscribed from `ScriptureBlock.semanticLabel`. Note the noun is `passage` and not `reading`: `reading` is this app's name for a whole day's session, which `/reading` is, and using it here would make the block's name and the screen's name the same word for two different things."
  },
  "@readingVerse": {
    "description": "The PREFIX for a verse marker's accessible name, as in `Verse 3`.\n\nDELIBERATELY **NOT** AN ICU PLURAL, and this is one of the two counts that are genuinely not plural-dependent. English never writes \"Verses 3\" for a single marker — the marker NAMES an index, it does not count a set — and the Arabic `الآية` takes an Arabic-Indic numeral the same way. A plural here would select an `other` branch identical to the `one` branch and assert that the two agree, which is a test that cannot fail. See `readingCaptionFor` for a count that IS."
  },
  "@readingRetry": {
    "description": "`ErrorView.retryLabel`. The prototype has no error state at all — `eva/src` has no data layer that can fail — so this is written. NAMESPACED `reading*`; see `homeRetry` for the three-way collision."
  },
  "@resultReflectAgain": {
    "description": "The primary button. Transcribed: ResultScreen.tsx:77."
  },
  "@resultBackHome": {
    "description": "The secondary button.\n\nWRITTEN, NOT TRANSCRIBED, AND TRANSCRIBING WOULD HAVE BEEN A LIE: the prototype's label is `Back to library` (ResultScreen.tsx:78) and there IS no library — AGENT_CONTEXT §2 decision 1 cut it, and the destination is `/`. The label says where the button actually goes."
  },
  "@resultThisAnswer": {
    "description": "The first stat tile: what this answer scored. Written, because the prototype's tile read `14 Read` and days-read is profile history with no endpoint behind it (§2 decision 1 cut the profile)."
  },
  "@resultBestRun": {
    "description": "The second stat tile: the reader's best run of days.\n\nTHERE IS NO THIRD TILE, and the reason is arithmetic rather than taste: the submit response has four drawable numbers and they are ALL used — on the headline, in the pill, and in these two tiles. The prototype's three tiles are profile history (days read, reflections, best score), and no endpoint could produce them. A third tile would repeat one of the other four."
  },
  "@resultDay": {
    "description": "The streak pill's leading noun. Transcribed: ResultScreen.tsx:65 — `Day 12`.\n\nDELIBERATELY **NOT** AN ICU PLURAL, and this is the judgement call in this file. The pill renders a LABEL FORM (`Day 12`, `اليوم ١٢`), not a count phrase: English `Day 12` is correct for every count including one, and Arabic `اليوم` with an Arabic-Indic numeral is the conventional label too. Making it a plural would render `Days 12` in English — ALTERING A TRANSCRIBED PROTOTYPE STRING to fix nothing — and would change the pill's visual shape on every screen that shows a streak.\n\nThe count that IS genuinely plural-dependent on this screen is `resultTotalCaption`, below. `readingCaptionFor` is the other. Every other count in the app was checked and recorded in AGENT_CONTEXT decision 104."
  },
  "@resultLongestYet": {
    "description": "Appended to the streak label when the reader is at their best. Transcribed: ResultScreen.tsx:65 — the ` — your longest yet` half of `Day 12 — your longest yet`.\n\nTHE COMPARISON IS THIS CLIENT'S, NOT THE PROTOTYPE'S: the prototype's own sentence sits beside `longest_streak: 6` with `Day 12`, a hard-coded mismatch in the fixture it drew. So the words are transcribed and the `>=` test is not. See `resultStreakLabelFor`'s Dart doc for why it is `>=` and not `==`, and why a streak of `0` never claims it."
  },
  "@resultCompleteMessage": {
    "description": "The headline sentence: right, and the reading is finished.\n\nTHREE MESSAGES AND NOT ONE, BECAUSE THE SERVER SENDS THREE STATES. `SubmitResult` carries `is_correct` AND `reading_completed`, and AGENT_CONTEXT §5 trap 4 says the streak fields only move on the second. A single \"Correct.\" would say the same thing whether or not the reader's day is done, which is the one thing this screen exists to tell them."
  },
  "@resultPartialMessage": {
    "description": "The headline sentence: right, and there is more to do. See `resultCompleteMessage`."
  },
  "@resultIncorrectMessage": {
    "description": "The headline sentence: the answer was wrong.\n\nWRITTEN, NOT TRANSCRIBED. `ResultScreen.tsx:50` ships `So close. One more read and you've got it.`, which is keyed on its own hard-coded `4/5` - and this client has neither number: `SubmitResult` carries `points_earned` and `current_total_points` and **no question count** (AGENT_CONTEXT 2's route table gives `/result` no repository), so a sentence about being 'one read away' would be a claim about a measurement that does not exist. This is decision 45's rejected 'invent a measurement', and it is the largest single divergence on the screen.\n\nThe consolation clause is deliberate and it is the product's, not the prototype's: a wrong answer on a five-question set is one of five, and saying so is true for every count the server can send."
  },
  "@resultTotalCaption": {
    "description": "The caption under the headline score. THIS IS A REAL ICU PLURAL, SELECTION ONLY.\n\nSELECTION ONLY, WITH NO `{digits}`, AND THAT IS THE POINT: the score itself is drawn by the page in `displayLarge` one line above, so folding the numeral in here would print it TWICE. What the count selects is the NOUN, which is exactly the thing that has to agree with it.\n\nWHY THE OLD BARE NOUN WAS AN ARABIC CORRECTNESS BUG. The value was `نقطة` — SINGULAR — for every score. `٥ نقطة` is wrong Arabic; 3-10 takes the plural `نقاط` and the dual is `نقطتان`. A noun that cannot agree with the number above it is the same class of defect as the Arabic-only plural gap `readingCaptionFor` records, and it is fixed here by the same mechanism.\n\nWHY THE ENGLISH OUTPUT DOES NOT CHANGE: every score in this app's tests and fixtures is greater than one, so the `other` branch ('points') is what was already rendered. `one{point}` is the branch that was MISSING.\n\nPASSED `result.currentTotalPoints`, the same integer the page renders above it — one count, one source.",
    "placeholders": {
      "count": {
        "type": "int",
        "example": "1234"
      }
    }
  },
  "@settingsTitle": {
    "description": "The screen's title, beside the back chevron. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:43 writes `Settings` in `F.display 26 / 600 / ink`.\n\nThe prototype is English-only — `eva/src` has one `SettingsScreen.tsx` and no Arabic variant — so the Arabic value is written, not transcribed."
  },
  "@settingsBack": {
    "description": "The header row's chevron's accessible name. Transcribed by position from SettingsScreen.tsx:39-42, which draws a bare `<button>` around an inline `<svg>` with **no label at all** — §14's first row, and the reason the name is a caller string rather than a design-system default.\n\nDeliberately the same word `readingBack` uses. Two features, one sentence: a back control is called Back in both, and a second wording would be a translation decision nobody made."
  },
  "@settingsAppearance": {
    "description": "The first section label. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:47 writes `Appearance` through its local `SectionLabel`, which `EvaSectionHeader` now renders.\n\nThe prototype's third group is `Account` (`:93`) and is CUT with the profile screen, so this screen has three groups where the prototype has four."
  },
  "@settingsTheme": {
    "description": "The theme picker's row title. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:50 writes `Theme`.\n\nThe prototype's picker is a `useState<'Light' | 'Dark' | 'System'>` whose initial value is `'Dark'`, which is why `UserSettings.themeMode`'s default is `dark` and not `system` — see `app_theme_mode.dart`'s doc."
  },
  "@settingsThemeLight": {
    "description": "One of the three segments on the theme track. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:52 writes `Light`.\n\nThe prototype renders the labels through `textTransform: 'uppercase'`, which Flutter has no equivalent for; `SegmentedControl` uppercases each label itself and is asserted on that. So the ARB value is the prototype's own casing and the widget changes it."
  },
  "@settingsThemeDark": {
    "description": "One of the three segments on the theme track. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:52 writes `Dark`.\n\nThis is the prototype's initial value (`useState<…>('Dark')`), so it is also the value a reader who has never opened this screen is in."
  },
  "@settingsThemeSystem": {
    "description": "One of the three segments on the theme track. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:52 writes `System`.\n\n`System` is NOT the app's default: `app_theme_mode.dart` records why it is dark instead, and this segment is the reader's way to ask for the OS's answer rather than the app's."
  },
  "@settingsFontSize": {
    "description": "The stepper's row title. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:62 writes `Font size`.\n\nNAMESPACED `settings*` rather than reused from `readingTextSize`, which is the `Aa` disclosure's tooltip on `/reading`. Same control, two positions on two screens, and the two widgets ask for their own string so a translator can move one without the other."
  },
  "@settingsFontScale": {
    "description": "The stepper track's slider label, announced as a slider. **Written, not transcribed**: SettingsScreen.tsx:66 is the prototype's `<input type=\"range\" min={1} max={5}>` and it carries **no accessible name at all**, so this is §14's first row applied to a control the row does not name.\n\nA distinct string from `settingsFontSize` because the two name the control from two positions — a row title and a slider — and `FontSizeStepperLabels`'s doc argues for exactly one value for its own three strings for the opposite reason."
  },
  "@settingsDecreaseFontSize": {
    "description": "The stepper's decrement button's tooltip and accessible name. **Written, not transcribed**: SettingsScreen.tsx:66 supplies its own stepper affordances on a native `<input type=\"range\">`, and a native input brings its own buttons with **no labels of its own**, so §14 required two names here that the prototype never wrote.\n\nDeliberately the same sentence as `readingDecreaseFontSize`: two screens draw the same control and a second wording would be a translation decision nobody made."
  },
  "@settingsIncreaseFontSize": {
    "description": "The stepper's increment button's tooltip and accessible name. **Written, not transcribed**, for `settingsDecreaseFontSize`'s reason: SettingsScreen.tsx:66's native range input carries its own `+` with no label.\n\nDeliberately the same sentence as `readingIncreaseFontSize`, for the same reason."
  },
  "@settingsReduceMotion": {
    "description": "The switch's row title. **Written, not transcribed — and there is no prototype row to transcribe from**: `SettingsScreen.tsx` has no motion row at all. This control exists because AGENT_CONTEXT §14 asks for reduced motion and `app.dart` recorded that a later phase would replace the platform-only default with the persisted `UserSettings` value. This is that phase.\n\nPlaced under APPEARANCE rather than READING because what it changes is how the app moves, not what it says."
  },
  "@settingsMotionOn": {
    "description": "The switch's accessible name while it is on. **Written, not transcribed**: `EvaToggle` shipped hard-coding the English words `On` and `Off` into its `Semantics` node for six phases, and no gate could see it because a semantics label is not painted — the Arabic glyph gate walks the painted tree, and a golden captures no semantics. Phase 9 made the switch's labels required and this is the shipped spelling.\n\nThe Arabic is a participle rather than a transliterated `On`, so the Arabic arm holds no Latin script and `app_localizations_test.dart`'s exception list does not grow."
  },
  "@settingsMotionOff": {
    "description": "The switch's accessible name while it is off. **Written, not transcribed**, as `settingsMotionOn` says — and `EvaToggle` is the widget that used to hard-code the English.\n\n`Off` and `On` are the words a screen reader has to distinguish to answer \"is animation on?\", so neither value may be empty and neither may be the other's translation of itself."
  },
  "@settingsReading": {
    "description": "The second section label. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:70 writes `Reading` through the same local `SectionLabel`.\n\nThis group ships ONE row of the prototype's two. `SettingsScreen.tsx:92`'s verse-numbers switch is cut for scope reasons recorded at length on `SettingsPage`, because `ScriptureBlock` has no `showVerseNumbers` parameter and its marker is load-bearing for four gates this phase does not own."
  },
  "@settingsDefaultLanguage": {
    "description": "The language row's title, and the row that opens the sheet. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:76 writes `Default language`.\n\nThis row is where **defect #7** is fixed, and the fix is not the row — the prototype's row was never broken. It is that the control writes `UserSettings.language` and `app.dart` installs it as `MaterialApp.locale`. The sheet is `features/settings/presentation/widgets/language_sheet.dart` and its doc records that the FAB this defect names was cut with the profile screen."
  },
  "@settingsLanguageSheetTitle": {
    "description": "The sheet's own heading. **Written, not transcribed** — SettingsScreen.tsx:77-90 draws the language pills inline and defines no sheet, no heading and no dismiss affordance at all. A bottom sheet needs a name for §14's first row, and inventing a row for it inside the screen is this client's own contribution.\n\nThe sentence is an instruction rather than a noun phrase because the sheet's two rows are both languages and neither can be named \"the language\"."
  },
  "@settingsLanguageEnglish": {
    "description": "The English row in the language sheet. Transcribed in **kind** from SettingsScreen.tsx:88, which writes the pill's own label as `l === 'EN' ? 'English' : 'العربية'` — so the prototype already draws one end of this pair in Arabic, which is where the Arabic form of `العربية` comes from. **Written** for the other end: the prototype has no `الإنجليزية` string anywhere, so that half is this client's own.\n\nThe Arabic value here is `الإنجليزية` and not `English`, deliberately: an Arabic reader who cannot read Latin would otherwise be unable to choose English, which is the one choice whose absence locks them out of the other arm. That decision also keeps `app_localizations_test.dart`'s `latinIsCorrect` exception list from growing, which an earlier draft of `settings_l10n.dart` claimed was not the case."
  },
  "@settingsLanguageArabic": {
    "description": "The Arabic row in the language sheet. **Transcribed**: SettingsScreen.tsx:88 writes the pill's label as `l === 'EN' ? 'English' : 'العربية'`, so `العربية` is the prototype's own string for this row and it is the evidence for the rule the whole file follows — a language names itself in its own script.\n\nThe prototype draws the two-letter codes `EN` and `AR` beside the names; this client draws the **names only**, because `EN` and `AR` are Latin script on the Arabic arm and the two codes are also exactly what `ReadingLanguage.code` already holds for the API path — so they are rendered from the domain enum rather than translated."
  },
  "@settingsAbout": {
    "description": "The third section label. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:107 writes `About` through the same local `SectionLabel`.\n\nThe group ships ONE row of the prototype's two: `SettingsScreen.tsx:118`'s Privacy policy is cut because it is a legal document with no URL, no screen and no endpoint, and an inert row that answers \"unavailable in this build\" is worse than no row at all for that particular question."
  },
  "@settingsPreferencesUnavailable": {
    "description": "The notice shown when `SettingsStatus` is `failed`. **Written, not transcribed**, because the prototype has nothing to transcribe from: `eva/src` has no data layer and therefore no error state anywhere, and `SettingsScreen.tsx` cannot fail because every value on it is a `useState` in the same file. `homeRetry` makes the same argument for the same reason.\n\nTHE SENTENCE IS **NOT** `Failure.message`, and that is the point of this key existing. Every other `ErrorView` in this app shows the mapper's wording — `error_view.dart` states that `ApiErrorMapper` is the only source of a displayable failure message, and that claim is true of all three of them. `/settings` is the fourth call site and the first **non-network** one: `SettingsRepositoryImpl._unreachable` builds `'The preferences could not be reached: $error'`, which interpolates the raw Dart exception, so rendering it would put a `MissingPluginException` on a reader's screen. A developer string and a reader's sentence are different things and only one of them is translated here.\n\nWRITTEN AS ONE SENTENCE COVERING BOTH FAILURE ARMS, because the cubit reports a failed READ and a failed WRITE through one status and the reader cannot tell from the screen which happened. 'Your preferences could not be saved' is true of both — the read failed, so what is on screen is not what is stored, which is the same fact from the other side."
  },
  "@settingsRetry": {
    "description": "The notice's action, which re-runs `SettingsCubit.load()`. **Written, not transcribed** — `SettingsScreen.tsx` has no error state and therefore no retry control, exactly as `homeRetry`, `quizRetry` and `readingRetry` do not.\n\nDeliberately the same sentence as those three, which is a pattern rather than a coincidence: `settingsBack` and `readingBack` share their wording for the same reason, and four independently-written keys saying 'Try again' in two languages is four translation decisions nobody made.\n\nNAMESPACED `settings*` because five features now carry this field name."
  },
  "@settingsVersion": {
    "description": "The version row's title. Transcribed: SettingsScreen.tsx:115 writes `Version`, with the value beside it.\n\nThe **value** is `AppConfig.appVersion`, not an ARB key, because it is not prose: it is `pubspec.yaml`'s own `version:` with a build suffix, and `settings_page_test.dart` parses `pubspec.yaml` and asserts the two agree. Putting it in this file would have been a number a translator could edit and nothing would check."
  },
  "sealFabOpen": "Open menu",
  "@sealFabOpen": {
    "description": "`ds.tsx:559-564`'s 56px circle carries only the text `E`, so it has no accessible name of its own and a screen reader reaches an unnamed button. This is the trigger's shut name, and it says what activating does rather than what the control is."
  },
  "sealFabClose": "Close menu",
  "@sealFabClose": {
    "description": "The same trigger while the dial is open. A toggling button needs two names, or the announcement describes the current state and not the action — which is the defect `SegmentedControl` shipped with."
  },
  "sealFabSettings": "Settings",
  "@sealFabSettings": {
    "description": "A `SealFab` destination. `ds.tsx:538` offers `Settings`; rejected was dropping it, since the avatar on `/` is a 32px target in a top bar and this is a labelled destination one tap away."
  },
  "sealFabLeaderboard": "Communion",
  "@sealFabLeaderboard": {
    "description": "A `SealFab` destination, and the reason this control exists. It occupies the prototype's `Profile` slot (`ds.tsx:537`): Profile is a cut screen with no endpoint, and the board is the only prototype destination with no other entry point on `/`.\n\n**The word is \"Communion\", and it is the redesign's own** — `eva/redesign.html:932`'s `dockBoard` row, the same word as the screen's title (`:615`'s `boardT`). It was \"Leaderboard\" until Task 5.5 renamed the route, and the Arabic arm had already moved to \"الشركة\" — so for a while the two arms of this one key named two different things."
  },
  "readingVerses": "verses",
  "@readingVerses": {
    "description": "`:966` — `verses:[\"verses\",\"آيات\"]`. The unit the crumb counts with (`${N(b.en.length)} ${tr('verses')}`, `:1346`); home owns the same word under `homeVerses` because the two screens compose different sentences."
  },
  "readingQuiz": "quiz",
  "@readingQuiz": {
    "description": "`:966` — `quiz:[\"quiz\",\"اختبار\"]`. The caption the CTA is built around (`:1347`), not a heading: the prototype writes `` `${tr('quiz')} · ${N(qEff)} ${tr('questions')}` ``."
  },
  "readingQuestions": "questions",
  "@readingQuestions": {
    "description": "`:966` — `questions:[\"questions\",\"أسئلة\"]`. The unit beside the CTA's numeral. **Not a plural**: the prototype renders one word for every count, exactly as home's `verseCount` and `selahSeconds` do."
  },
  "readingListen": "listen",
  "@readingListen": {
    "description": "`:965` — `listen:[\"listen\",\"استمع\"]`. The pill's last button (`#listen`, `:559`)."
  },
  "readingSky": "sky",
  "@readingSky": {
    "description": "`:968` — `sky:[\"sky\",\"سماء\"]`. The crumb's third clause (`` `${tr('sky')} ${N(skyN)}` ``, `:1346`) — the count of lit stars, which is `SkyState.count`."
  },
  "readingBookmarkOn": "bookmarked",
  "@readingBookmarkOn": {
    "description": "`:974` — `bmk:[\"✦ bookmarked\",\"✦ تم الحفظ\"]`, and the leading `✦` is **deliberately dropped**: U+2726 is in no bundled family (measured — `arabic_typography_test.dart` failed with `[10022]` on the selah glyph before it became a painted star), so a toast carrying it would ship a tofu box. The glyph is decoration on a transient label; the word is the information. `readingBookmark` keeps the button's own name, which `:544` writes as `title=\"bookmark\"` with no glyph at all."
  },
  "readingBookmarkOff": "bookmark removed",
  "@readingBookmarkOff": {
    "description": "`:974` — `bmkOff:[\"bookmark removed\",\"أُزيل الحفظ\"]`. The toggle's off label; a bookmark that reports only the on state cannot be turned off blind."
  },
  "journeyTitle": "Journey",
  "journeyScribe": "scribe · level 7",
  "journeyStreakSub": "day streak · keep the flame",
  "journeyRingVerses": "verses",
  "journeyRingQuizzes": "quizzes",
  "journeyRingAccuracy": "accuracy",
  "journeySky": "sky",
  "journeyStarsLit": "stars lit",
  "journeyConstellations": "constellations",
  "journeyMedals": "medals",
  "journeyMedalFirstLight": "first light",
  "journeyMedalPsalmist": "psalmist",
  "journeyMedalStreakSeven": "streak ×7",
  "journeyMedalJohn": "john",
  "journeyAccount": "account",
  "journeyRowBoard": "The Communion",
  "journeyRowSettings": "Settings",
  "journeyRowShare": "Share progress",
  "journeyRowPlans": "Reading plans",
  "journeyUnavailableSuffix": "unavailable in this build",
  "@journeyTitle": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:933` — the i18n row `journey`, rendered by `:579`'s `.ttl`.\n\nThe screen's own name, and it is the redesign's name for the **profile**: ADR-0022 restored the profile ADR-0001 cut, and the redesign calls it \"Journey\". The route is `/journey` for the same reason."
  },
  "@journeyScribe": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:933` — the i18n row `scribe`, rendered by `:588`'s `.jrole`.\n\n**The level is an unconditioned literal and there is no levels endpoint.** The backend ships fourteen routes and none of them is a level, a rank or a title; the prototype writes `scribe · level 7` and never changes it. So the whole line is transcribed copy, and the \"7\" is **not** derived from anything — recorded as a divergence at the widget.\n\n**The Arabic arm embeds the Arabic-Indic `٧` in the source string** (the prototype's own row, `:933`), so it is a literal in the ARB rather than a `digits()` call at the call site."
  },
  "@journeyStreakSub": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:934` — the i18n row `streakSub`, rendered by `:590`.\n\nThe line under the hero number. The hero number above it is the reader's **real** `current_streak` (`GET /streak/summary`); this line is the prototype's own copy."
  },
  "@journeyRingVerses": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:935` — the i18n row `rlVerses`, rendered by `:592`.\n\n**The label is the prototype's, and the quantity behind it is not.** The backend counts **questions**, not verses (`streak.service.ts:5-17` carries `total_questions` / `answered_questions` / `correct_answers` and no verse count at all), so the ring under this label is a **scheduled-day completion ratio**. The divergence is recorded at the derivation, and it is the same shape as recorded decision 27's \"beads count questions\"."
  },
  "@journeyRingQuizzes": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:935` — the i18n row `rlQuizzes`, rendered by `:593`.\n\n**This ring is a count, and it is the only one that is.** The prototype writes `data-v=\"86\"` with **no** percent sign where its two neighbours write `83%` and `92%` — an inconsistency in the prototype's own markup, resolved **deliberately** rather than transcribed: the quantity is the number of questions answered, so a percent sign on it would be a unit the number does not have."
  },
  "@journeyRingAccuracy": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:935` — the i18n row `rlAcc`, rendered by `:594`.\n\nCorrect answers over answered questions, across the thirty-day history the endpoint returns."
  },
  "@journeySky": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:968` — the i18n row `sky`, rendered by `:596`."
  },
  "@journeyStarsLit": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:968` — the i18n row `stars`, rendered by `:596`.\n\n**Real.** `SkyRepository.count()` — the port's own doc already names `journey` as the consumer of it, and the prototype's `jSkyN` (`:596`) is written by `updateSkyWidget` (`:1186-1187`) from the same live star set."
  },
  "@journeyConstellations": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:968` — the i18n row `consts`, rendered by `:596`.\n\n**The prototype's own meaning is \"books whose final verse was lit\"** (`constDone`, `:1132`, set at `:1156-1157`). This client has no book-completion record, so the count is a **proxy** — the distinct books among the completed scheduled days — and the proxy is recorded at the derivation with what it gives up."
  },
  "@journeyMedals": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:936` — the i18n row `medals`, rendered by `:597`'s `.car-label`."
  },
  "@journeyMedalFirstLight": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:937` — the i18n row `m1`, rendered by `:599`'s `.mn`.\n\n**One of four medals the prototype draws as static markup.** The four medals, their glyphs and their count are ADR-0022's fiction: \"No screen may render a hard-coded … medal …; the fiction lives in data sources\". The **names** are copy and live here; **which** medals exist, and how many of the twelve are earned, come from the medals port."
  },
  "@journeyMedalPsalmist": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:937` — the i18n row `m2`, rendered by `:600`'s `.mn`.\n\nThe only one of the four whose emblem is a **typographic** glyph — `❧` (U+2767), which the `cmap` measurement puts in **EBGaramond only**. It is therefore drawn in the scripture family, pinned, on both arms."
  },
  "@journeyMedalStreakSeven": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:937` — the i18n row `m3`, rendered by `:601`'s `.mn`.\n\nThe `×` is U+00D7, which is in all five bundled families. The `7` is Latin in the prototype's own string; the Arabic arm is the prototype's `٧ أيام`."
  },
  "@journeyMedalJohn": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:937` — the i18n row `m4`, rendered by `:602`'s `.mn`."
  },
  "@journeyAccount": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:938` — the i18n row `account`, rendered by `:604`'s `.car-label`."
  },
  "@journeyRowBoard": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:939` — the i18n row `rowBoard`, rendered by `:605`'s `.srow`.\n\nThe board's own title — the same word the screen it navigates to uses (`leaderboardTitle`, `eva/redesign.html:939`'s `commG` row)."
  },
  "@journeyRowSettings": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:938` — the i18n row `rowSet`, rendered by `:606`'s `.srow`.\n\n**The app bar's gear button names the same destination**, so the tooltip and the row share this one string rather than two that can drift."
  },
  "@journeyRowShare": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:938` — the i18n row `rowShare`, rendered by `:607`'s `.srow`.\n\n**The prototype has no handler for this row either** — `el('rowShare')` appears nowhere in the prototype's script, so the button is rendered-and-inert there too. Here it is rendered-**and-disabled**, with the reason in its accessible name, because §14 asks a reader to be told *why* a control cannot be pressed."
  },
  "@journeyRowPlans": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html:938` — the i18n row `rowPlans`, rendered by `:608`'s `.srow`.\n\n**No handler in the prototype, and no endpoint in the backend** — the fourteen routes include nothing for reading plans. Same treatment as `journeyRowShare`: rendered-and-disabled, with the reason in the accessible name."
  },
  "@journeyUnavailableSuffix": {
    "description": "Appended to a control's accessible name when the control is **inert** — and the\nreason is that the feature is not built in this client.\n\n§14's disabled row: a reader has to be told WHY a control cannot be pressed, not only that it cannot. `LoginPage`'s four inert social buttons and `/`'s avatar are the precedent, and they are the reason /quiz can ship a dead CTA honestly at all.\n\n**The fifth of the `*UnavailableSuffix` keys**, and it reads like the four that say the same thing — `authUnavailableSuffix`, `homeUnavailableSuffix`, `readingUnavailableSuffix` and this one all read \"unavailable in this build\" — because the reason is the same: the feature is not built in this client. `quizUnavailableSuffix` reads differently because its control is dead for a different reason."
  },
  "journeyRetry": "Try again",
  "@journeyRetry": {
    "description": "`eva/redesign.html` has no retry on the journey — the prototype's screen has no\ndata layer to fail — so this is the **fifth** of the per-screen `*Retry` keys\nand it reads like the other four.\n\n**The repository namespaces `retry` per screen on purpose**, and this key is the\nsixth instance of that rule rather than an exception to it: `homeRetry`,\n`quizRetry`, `readingRetry`, `settingsRetry` and `leaderboardRetry` are five\nseparate keys with the same value, and the journey's screen borrowing the\nboard's would have been the first screen to take another screen's copy.\n\nSee `app_localizations_test.dart` for the assertion that keeps the family\nhonest — it asserts the *shape* (six of them, five of which say the same\nthing) rather than that they are all equal, because a test that demanded\nthey differ would be satisfied by a translator harmonising them and would\nbe wrong."
  }
}
